.TH TARION 1 "" "tarion" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tarion \- peer\-to\-peer, end\-to\-end encrypted terminal chat
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tarion
[\fIcommand\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tarion sends chat messages directly between devices over QUIC with mutually authenticated TLS 1.3.
A directory server (\fBtariond\fR(8)) tells peers where to find each other and helps them through home routers.
Each device has its own Ed25519 identity key; contacts pin it on first contact.
Without a command, \fBtarion\fR runs \fBstart\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBstart\fR [options]
open the menu and chat; listens itself if no background process runs
.TP
\fBmenu\fR
open the menu (same as start)
.TP
\fBbackground\fR [\-\-detach] [options]
run the always\-on listener; \-\-detach survives closing the terminal
.TP
\fBstop\fR
stop the background process
.TP
\fBsend\fR <name> <message>
send one message through the background process
.TP
\fBlisten\fR
print incoming messages and events as JSON lines (for scripts and apps)
.TP
\fBhistory\fR [\-n N] <name>
print a chat's recent history
.TP
\fBdevices\fR [approve CODE | remove NAME]
list your devices, add one with its code, or take one off
.TP
\fBprotect\fR [passphrase | windows | off]
show or change how your local data is encrypted
.TP
\fBportmap\fR
ask the router to forward your port again, and explain the result
.TP
\fBfirewall\fR [allow | remove]
check Windows Firewall; allow lets people reach you
.TP
\fBoutbox\fR
list messages waiting to be delivered
.TP
\fBdropped\fR [\-n N]
list messages that were given up on or refused
.TP
\fBid\fR
show your peer ID so contacts can verify you
.TP
\fBpeers\fR
list pinned contacts and their peer IDs
.TP
\fBforget\fR <name>
forget a contact's pinned key (after they reinstall)
.TP
\fBpasswd\fR
change your account password
.TP
\fBdelete\-account\fR
delete your account on the server
.TP
\fBhelp\fR [command | topic]
show help: tarion help devices, tarion help relay, ...
.SH OPTIONS
These options are shared by \fBstart\fR, \fBmenu\fR and \fBbackground\fR. Options marked "saved" are written to config.json.
.TP
\fB\-accept\fR
who may message you: anyone or contacts (saved)
.TP
\fB\-cp\fR
local TCP port of the background control socket (default: \-p plus 10000)
.TP
\fB\-invite\fR
one\-time invite code, if the server is invite\-only
.TP
\fB\-mailbox\fR
off (default) or on: let the server hold sealed messages while you or a contact are offline (saved)
.TP
\fB\-p\fR
local UDP port for chat and server traffic (default 63426)
.TP
\fB\-pass\fR
directory password (only needed the first time on this device)
.TP
\fB\-port\-mapping\fR
auto (default: ask the router to forward the port when the relay is off), on, or off (saved)
.TP
\fB\-public\-addr\fR
address peers can reach you at directly, e.g. a forwarded port "203.0.113.7:63426"; "none" clears it (saved)
.TP
\fB\-relay\fR
off (default) or on: allow messages through the server when no direct path exists (saved)
.TP
\fB\-server\fR
directory server host:port (tariond default port 63425)
.TP
\fB\-server\-bans\fR
on (default) or off: refuse keys the server banned (saved)
.TP
\fB\-user\fR
directory username
.TP
\fB\-\-detach\fR
(background) start detached, so it survives closing the terminal.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR \fIname\fR \fB\-i\fR \fIip:port\fR
(start) chat directly with an address, without a server.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR
(history, dropped) how many recent lines to show; 0 shows all.
